## A B S T R A C T
Corrosion of metallic materials is a long-standing problem in many engineering fields. Various organic coatings have been widely applied in anticorrosion of metallic materials over the past decades. However, the protective performance of many organic coatings is limited due to the undesirable local failure of the coatings caused by micro-pores and cracks in the coating matrix. Recently, metal organic frameworks (MOFs)-based surfaces and coatings (MOFBSCs) have exhibited great potential in constructing protective materials on metallic substrates with efficient and durable anticorrosion performance. The tailorable porous structure, flexible composition, numerous active sites, and controllable release properties of MOFs make them an ideal platform for developing various protective functionalities, such as self-healing property, superhydrophobicity, and physical barrier against corrosion media. MOFs-based anticorrosion surfaces and coatings can be divided into two categories: the composite surfaces/coatings using MOFs-based passive/active nanofillers and the surfaces/coatings using MOFs as functional substrate support. In this work, the state-of-the-art fabrication strategies of the MOFBSCs are systematically reviewed. The anticorrosion mechanisms of MOFBSCs and functions of the MOFs in the coating matrix are discussed accordingly. Additionally, we highlight both traditional and emerging electrochemical techniques for probing protective performances and mechanisms of MOFBSCs. The remaining challenging issues and perspectives are also discussed.
## 1. Introduction
Metal and alloy are widely used as engineering equipment in marine, gas, oil, and aerospace industries because of their excellent mechanical strength, processability, and wide sources. However, most metallic materials are vulnerable to the corrosive environment. The corrosion of metals can lead to tremendous economic loss, environmental pollution, and potential safety hazard. Globally, the cost relating to corrosion amounts to $\$2.5$ trillion every year, accounting for $3\text{\textperthousand}$ of the gross domestic product of industrialized countries [1,2]. Many techniques are utilized for protecting metallic materials against corrosion, such as surface treatment [3,4], electrochemical protection [5,6], corrosion in­ hibitors, and protective coatings [7,8]. However, the unrestrained use of electrochemical protection and corrosion inhibitors will cause consid­ erable expense and environmental pollution [9,10]. Constructing func­ tional surfaces and coatings on metallic substrates have been proven to be an efficient, economical, and scalable strategy in protection of metallic materials, which usually serve as a physical barrier to avoid the direct contact of metallic substrates with aggressive components in the environment, such as the dissolved oxygen, corrosive ions (e.g., chloride anion), and water. The anticorrosion performance of functional surfaces and coatings principally depends on the impermeability of the surfaces/ coatings. The conventional coatings based on chromium, phosphate, and vanadium are gradually eliminated because they are environmentally hazardous. Recently, organic coatings with superior adhesion, me­ chanical properties, and electrical insulation performance (e.g., epoxy resin, polyurethane, and acrylic resin) have been widely applied in anticorrosion of metallic materials. However, within the long service duration, the corrosion species can gradually penetrate these coatings through the micro-defects and weaken the protective effect of these coatings. Herein, it is urgent to develop novel functional surfaces and coatings with more efficient and durable anticorrosion performance.
With the improved insights into the physiochemical properties of nanomaterials, anticorrosion surfaces/coatings based on functional nanomaterials have been proposed and investigated extensively. The nanomaterials could endow the anticorrosion surfaces/coatings with various unique features, such as enhanced impermeability, self-healing, and special surface properties. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of porous crystalline nanomaterials assembled by connecting metal-based nodes (metal ions or clusters) and organic ligands into extended networks [11]. MOFs have been employed in a variety of ap­ plications, such as catalysis [12], gas storage and separation [13], as well as energy storage and conversion [14], owing to their ultra-high porosity, tunable morphology and pore size, well-exposed functional sites, and diverse compositions [15]. In the field of anticorrosion, MOFs were firstly used as corrosion inhibitors for some metallic substrates due to their high surface areas, wide supramolecular characters, and abun­ dance of functional groups that can facilitate their adsorption on metal surfaces [16]. More recently, the potential of MOFs in the construction of corrosion protective surfaces and coatings has been gradually exploited. Unlike some traditional nanomaterials which only serve as the passive nanofillers blocking the diffusion paths of corrosion media in the organic coatings, the versatility of MOFs offers a great opportunity to develop adaptive materials with multiple anticorrosion effects to protect metallic substrates against diverse corrosive components [17,18].
In this review, we summarize the up-to-date advances in the fabri­ cation of metal organic frameworks-based surfaces and coatings (MOFBSCs) for the corrosion protection of metallic materials (Fig. 1). The protective mechanisms of the MOFBSCs, functions of the MOFs in the coating matrix, and electrochemical techniques for characterizing MOFBSCs are also discussed. Firstly, we introduce the anticorrosion surfaces/coatings using MOFs-based nanofillers. MOFs-based nanofillers can enhance the protective performance of organic coatings from two aspects: blocking the diffusion paths of corrosion media and releasing healing agents by external stimuli. Meanwhile, many important factors influencing the anticorrosion properties of MOFs-based nanofillers are discussed, such as compatibility to coating matrix, dispersibility, and electrical conductivity. Secondly, the anticorrosion surfaces/coatings using MOFs directly as functional substrate support are discussed. In these surfaces/coatings, MOFs correspondingly serve as the textured substrates for superhydrophobic surfaces (SHSs) and nano-reservoir of lubricant for slippery liquid infused porous surfaces (SLIPSs). The methods for the enhancement of robustness and durability of these surfaces and coatings are also discussed. Thirdly, frequently used elec­ trochemical techniques for the characterization of MOFBSCs are intro­ duced. Among these techniques, the traditional electrochemical techniques such as open circuit potential (OCP), electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), and Tafel polarization curves can be used for the evaluation of anticorrosion performances of the protective sur­ faces/coatings, and the emerging localized electrochemical techniques, including scanning Kelvin probe (SKP), scanning vibrating electrode technique (SVET), scanning electrochemical microscope (SECM), and local electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (LEIS), are very useful methods to probe the anticorrosion mechanisms of MOFBSCs. The remaining challenging issues and perspectives for future studies of MOFBSCs are also discussed.
## 2. Anticorrosion surfaces/coatings using MOFs-based nanofillers
Organic coatings like epoxy resin, polyurethane, and acrylic resin are frequently used as retarding materials for corrosion protection of metals and alloys owing to their excellent chemical resistance, wear-resistance, and adhesion to substrates. Generally, organic coatings are served as a physical barrier layer against corrosive media $\mathrm{Cl}^{-},\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ). However, these organic coatings cannot completely prevent the permeation of the corrosive media because the evaporation of solvent at the curing process of the coatings and the external factors during the service period can inevitably cause the formation of micro-pores and cracks in the coating matrix. The corrosive media $(\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $|0_{2}\rangle$ can gradually penetrate the coating through these defects and then contact with metal substrates and initiate the corrosion of metals. Ultimately, the organic coatings will lose
their protective functions.
To overcome this drawback, an effective way is to incorporate functional nanofillers into organic coatings to enhance the protective performance. The nanofillers for anticorrosion composite coatings can be classified as passive and active categories, respectively, according to different anticorrosion mechanisms of the nanofillers in the organic coatings.
## 2.1. MOFs-based passive nanofillers: blocking the diffusion paths of corrosion media
Passive nanofillers can block the defects in the composite coatings and decrease the diffusion paths of aggressive species, which signifi­ cantly improve the barrier property and extend the service time of the composite coatings. Various nanoparticles have been utilized as passive nanofillers over the past decades, such as $\mathrm{SiO_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C a C O}_{3}$ , and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ With the booming development of two-dimensional (2D) nanomaterials, many 2D functional passive nanofillers, like hexagonal boron nitride (hBN), transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs), graphite carbon nitride, graphene, and graphene derivatives were proposed [19]. Generally, these 2D passive nanofillers with lamellar structure can more effectively improve the barrier property of the composite coatings compared to nanoparticles. More specifically, the 2D passive nanofillers can form the “labyrinth effect” in the coating matrix, which substantially reduce the diffusion paths of the corrosion media [20]. The design and synthesis of passive nanofillers are focused on improving the compatibility of nanofillers with organic coating matrix since the poor compatibility could result in serious agglomeration of nanofillers in the coating, which is detrimental to the shielding effect of the composite coating. In other words, the well-dispersed nanofillers will further enhance the barrier property of composite coatings. Many types of MOFs have excellent compatibility with various organic coatings due to the active sites (functional groups) in organic linkers of MOFs that are able to form covalent bonds or non-covalent interactions (e.g., hydrogen bonds, $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction, coordination, and hydrophobic interactions) with func­ tional groups in the coating matrix. 2D MOFs nanofillers with lamellar structure, abundant active sites, and unique chemical composition could simultaneously bring excellent impermeability and compatibility to the coating matrix. For instance, Qiu et al. developed an ultrathin Cu-MOFs nanosheet (Cu-TCPP) with excellent water stability via surfactantassisted method (Fig. 2). The Cu-TCPP nanosheets have been used as passive nanofillers for epoxy coatings. The Cu-TCPP nanosheets/epoxy composite coating can significantly prevent the penetration of water, and the anticorrosion performance was better than that of the pure epoxy and the epoxy composite coating incorporated with bulk CuTCPP, as the uniformly dispersed Cu-TCPP nanosheets effectively reduced the permeation paths of corrosion media [21].
MOFs can be used as the surface modification material for other passive nanofillers. The abundant functional groups of MOFs can change the surface properties of nanocomposites and improve the interfacial compatibility of nanocomposites with organic coating matrix. Taking graphene and its derivatives as an example, the graphene-based nano­ fillers have impressive impermeability to corrosion media. However, graphene and its derivatives are exceedingly easy to form aggregates in the organic coatings because of the van der Waals forces between the graphene layers that leads to their self-aggregation behavior and poor compatibility of pristine graphene with organic coatings [22,23]. More seriously, once the corrosion media arrive at the interface of metal/ coating, graphene would form galvanic pair with metal substrate and accelerate the corrosion process due to the conductivity of graphene [24,25]. MOFs can be easily decorated onto the surface of graphene oxides (GO) as an interfacial compatibility assistant to improve the dispersion of nanofillers [26]. In addition, the MOFs wrapping layer is able to act as an insulator to isolate the conductive graphene from directly contacting with metal substrates, which avoids the occurrence of galvanic corrosion [27–29]. Very recently, Ramezanzadeh et al. re­ ported a ZIF-8 decorated graphene oxide (GO) composite nanofiller $(\mathbf{G}\mathbf{O}@\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{F}{-}8)$ for epoxy coating (Fig. 3). The low-frequency impedance values of ZIF-8/epoxy and GO@ZIF-8/epoxy were higher than $10^{10}\Omega$ $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ during the whole immersion period, while the neat epoxy and GO/ epoxy coatings showed a significant decrease in low frequency imped­ ance values, suggesting the excellent barrier performances of ZIF-8/ epoxy and $\operatorname{GO}(\mathcal{Q}$ ZIF-8/epoxy coatings. This was mainly attributed to the imidazole groups on the surface of ZIF-8 and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{G O}}({\overline{{a}}}){\mathsf{Z I F}}-8$ that can participate into the curing reaction of the epoxy matrix, prominently improving the dispersion of ZIF-8 and GO/ZIF-8 as well as the compactness of the corresponding epoxy composite coatings [30].
In summary, MOFs-based passive nanofillers can be mainly divided into two categories, the pure MOFs and the MOFs-decorated nano­ composites. Apart from the reduction of micro-pores in the coating matrix, the MOFs-based passive nanofillers can further enhance the barrier property of composite coatings by improving the compatibility and dispersion of nanofillers in the composite coatings, benefited from abundant functional groups in the organic linkers of MOFs.
## 2.2. MOFs-based active nanofillers: releasing healing agents by external stimuli
Active nanofillers can automatically repair the defects in the undermined composite coatings and restore the protective effect of the coatings to some extent, which is one of strategies for constructing selfhealing anticorrosion coatings. Compared to the passive nanofillers which mainly block the micro-pores and reduce the diffusion paths of corrosion media in the coating matrix, the active nanofillers usually work after the local failure occurs in the coating. Originally, the use of active nanofillers is to directly incorporate some healing agents (e.g., corrosion inhibitors and reactive polymers). When the coating is destroyed, these healing agents can actively replenish the defects and prolong the protective period of the coatings. Nevertheless, these heal­ ing agents are usually soluble or reactive. Thus, direct doping of these healing agents into the coating matrix can deteriorate the entirety of coatings, while the undesirable leakage of healing agents from the coating is also unavoidable. In recent years, the nanocontainers loaded with healing agents have been considered as a type of ideal active nanofiller for constructing self-healing anticorrosion coatings [31]. When the coating is damaged, these nanocontainers can respond to the external stimuli, like pH, specific ions, light, corrosion potential, tem­ perature, etc., and automatically release the pre-loaded healing agents to repair the defects (Fig. 4) [32–36]. There are several key points in the design of nanocontainers: (1) sufficient loading capacity; (2) high sensitivity (viz. the nanocontainer that can realize “zero leakage” when the coating is not destroyed and release healing agents promptly when required, which is the premise to make full use of healing agents and achieve long-term protection performance of the coating); (3) superior compatibility with coating matrix (viz. the nanocontainer is able to be uniformly dispersed in the composite coating since the aggregation of nanocontainers in the coating will seriously deteriorate the integrity of the coating and leave some sites of the coating without self-repairing capability) [35]. Numerous porous and multi-layered nanomaterials have been developed as nanocontainers for self-healing coatings, like mesoporous silicas (MSNs) [37,38], hollow carbon spheres [39], gra­ phene derivatives [7,40], etc. However, in order to realize controllable release of cargoes in response to external stimuli and uniform dispersion of nanocontainers, the surficial functionalization of these materials usually requires a series of organic synthesis and purification, which is complicated and time-consuming.
As mentioned in Section 2.1, MOFs usually have excellent compati­ bility to organic coating matrix due to the diverse interaction between the organic linkers of MOFs and coating matrix. Additionally, the abundant pores in MOFs can be utilized to encapsulate many different healing agents. Furthermore, as a complex consists of metal ions and organic linkers, MOFs can be decomposed by some external stimuli from the environment, because there are many competitive equilibriums be­ tween different ionic species and the organic ligands, the complex al­ ways tends to transform into a more stable state, which facilitate the controllable release of the loaded cargoes. Based on all of the abovementioned characteristics of MOFs, it is reasonable to design MOFsbased active nanofillers with both compatibility to coating matrix, controllable release property under external stimuli, and high loading capacity.
MOFs-based active nanofillers can be classified into two types, the MOFs nanocontainers and composite nanofillers using MOFs as stimuliresponsive gatekeepers. For the former, healing agents are directly encapsulated in the cavity and pores of MOFs, and MOFs simultaneously serve as carrier of cargos and controller of release. In the later case, the healing agents are loaded in other porous materials and MOFs only act as the nanovalves on the surface of nanocomposites for the controllable release. Additionally, MOFs are also vital template materials in the fabrication process of the MOFs-based active nanofillers.
The general preparation strategy of MOFs nanocontainers is a twostep route, including the synthesis of MOFs and the subsequent adsorption process of healing agents with stirring under vacuum. However, the molecule sizes of the cargoes used in this strategy were commonly smaller than the pores of the selected MOFs, so the leakage of healing agents during the rinsing process and service period is un­ avoidable. Besides, in order to enhance the affinity of the healing agents to the MOFs and increase the loading capacity of the healing agents, the polarity of healing agents should be similar to the core of the MOFs.
In-situ preparation strategy as one of the most efficient methods can either directly encapsulate healing agents into the nanochannels of MOFs during the forming process of MOFs, or synthesize healing agents from small molecules to larger components in the cavity of as-prepared MOFs [41]. The ligand exchange method is an efficient and facile way for in-situ preparation of MOFs nanocontainers. MOFs are composed of metal nodes and organic linkers, some healing agents which usually contain N, O, and S elements (e.g., benzotriazole, n-butylamine, and 1- methylimidazole) that can act as modulating ligands to compete with the organic linkers of the target MOFs and first coordinate with metal ions. Afterward, these healing agents are able to be substituted by the organic linkers of the target MOFs, directly forming the MOFs nano­ containers where the healing agents are left in pores or covered on the surface of the MOFs (Fig. 5A) [42,43]. Moreover, the concentration, basicity of the modulating ligands and strength of coordination bond between the metal sites and modulating ligand (healing agents) have intimate influences on the crystal size of the MOFs nanocontainers, which is a facile way to regulate the size of nanocontainers [44,45].
Another in-situ preparation method is inherent intercalation which has been widely utilized for constructing various kinds of corrosion inhibitor-encapsulated nanocontainers, especially in the field of layered double hydroxides (LDHs) nanocontainers [46,47]. Employing this method, MOFs-based nanocontainers could be prepared by directly doping organic or inorganic corrosion inhibitors into the mixture of metal sources and organic linkers. After the nucleation of MOFs, abun­ dance of corrosion inhibitors will be left in the core and nanochannels of the MOFs without further encapsulation steps. For example, the metal salt includes a metal cation that could participate in nucleation of MOFs and an anion with anticorrosion effects, when the metal cation co­ ordinates with organic linkers to form MOFs, the anionic corrosion in­ hibitors will be retained in the MOFs. Ren et al. prepared an epoxy composite coating using the zinc gluconate $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{nG}})$ encapsulated ZIF-8 nanocontainers (ZnG@ZIF-8). The ZnG@ZIF-8 epoxy composite coating manifested more excellent anticorrosion performance than the pure epoxy coating due to the in-situ encapsulated ZnG corrosion inhibitors in ZIF-8 and uniform dispersion of ZnG@ZIF-8 nanocontainers in the epoxy matrix (Fig. 5B) [48].
In many cases, small molecular components or monomers can be readily introduced into the nanochannels of MOFs. Then, in-situ reac­ tion and polymerization of these small molecular cargoes occur in the nanochannels of MOFs to obtain a nanocomposite. Typically, conductive polymers have great potential in anticorrosion due to their good adhe­ sion to the substrate, electrochemical activity, and non-toxicity. Taking polyaniline (PANI) as an example, the small aniline monomers can be insitu polymerized in the pores of MIL-101 and the structure-controlled PANI fills the nanochannels of the MIL-101, which solves the loading problem of PANI [49]. Cerium species are also effective corrosion in­ hibitors for various metal substrates. Motamedi et al. synthesized a Ceimidazole network (CIN) via a co-precipitation method (Fig. 5C). The excess $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ could be oxidized into $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ during its coordination with imidazole due to the existence of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and get a nanoceria-decorated CIN nanocontainer (NC/CIN). The NC/CIN nanocontainer was then applied to the epoxy matrix. The Ce species and imidazole part were proven to provide synergetic protection effects for the defected areas in the epoxy coating [50].
MOFs are great candidates to serve as self-sacrificial templates for construction of hollow porous materials and responsive gatekeepers for core-shell composite nanofillers. Both self-sacrificial templates and responsive gatekeepers are significant to develop active nanofillers for anticorrosion and other molecular delivery applications. As for the procedures of fabricating hollow porous materials, the metal ions and organic linkers first transform into the MOFs. The MOFs with tunable size and morphology are uniformly distributed as templates, and then the MOFs templates are coated with other materials to form a core-shell nanocomposite. Finally, the hollow porous materials are generated by an etching process of MOFs. Compared with those hard templates (e.g. polystyrene) which need a calcination post-treatment to be removed, the MOFs self-sacrificial templates are much more convenient to be utilized in fabrication of hollow nanocarrier [51,52].
Moreover, MOFs have been used as a stimuli-responsive gatekeeper coating on the surface of other porous materials loaded with healing agents, which benefit the controllable release of healing agents [53–55]. The acid or alkaline condition is the most common stimuli to trigger the decomposition of MOFs gatekeepers. On the one hand, the corrosion process of metals will generate abundant micro-anode and microcathode at the defect area, where the micro-anodic area is generally acidic and the micro-cathodic area is alkaline because of the hydrolysis of dissolved metal cation and the oxygen reduction reaction, respec­ tively. On the other hand, the coordination bonds in the MOFs will be influenced by acidic and alkaline conditions. Specifically, the $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ (lewis acid) usually competes with the metal ions of MOFs (lewis acid) to form a complex with an organic linker (lewis base), and the organic linker will be protonated. Besides, some metal ions of MOFs can be converted into ${\bf{M}}(O H)_{\mathrm{{n}}}$ by $\mathrm{OH}^{-}[56]$ . Zhou et al. made full use of ZIF-8 and synthesized the composite nanofillers (HMSN-BTA $@$ ZIF-8) using ZIF-8 as the selfsacrificial template for the fabrication of hollow mesoporous silica nanoparticles (HMSN), pH-responsive gatekeeper for controlling the release of corrosion inhibitors, and interfacial compatibility assistant to the solvent-based epoxy matrix (Fig. 6). The nanofillers could be uni­ formly dispersed in the epoxy matrix because the imidazole groups in the ZIF-8 can covalently interact with the epoxy groups in the resin, which dominantly enhanced the compatibility of HMSN-BTA@ZIF-8 active nanofillers to the epoxy matrix. The epoxy/HMSN-BTA $@$ ZIF-8 composite coatings exhibited excellent passive barrier and active selfhealing anticorrosion performances [57]. Microbiological corrosion (MC) is another indispensable problem in the practical engineering. Especially for the sulfate-reducing bacteria (SRB), which is the most common corrosive bacterium in the marine environment [58]. The respiration of SRB can induce intracellular sulfate reduction that con­ verts sulfate into sulfide ion $(S^{2-})$ . The generated $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{S}}^{2-}$ can form stable metal sulfides, which accelerates the corrosion process of metal sub­ strates. Zhang and co-workers also developed a similar active nanofiller. ZIF-8 was coated on the biocides (metronidazole, MNZ) loaded HMSN while MNZ-HMSN@ZIF-8 nanofillers were incorporated into the waterborne alkyd resin for the protection of carbon steels in the SRB-rich corrosive environment. $s^{2-}$ supplied by metabolization of SRB could readily combine with ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ in the ZIF-8 to form stable ZnS $(\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{sp}}=1.2\times$ ${10}^{-23}\mathrm{\overline{{\cdot}}}$ ) and trigger the release of MNZ biocides, which significantly reduced the probability of MC [59].
Interaction between various organic ligands and metal ions of MOFs can be utilized to the surface modification of MOFs-based active nano­ fillers for further enhancing the compatibility in the coating matrix. The ligand exchange method is also a useful strategy for surface modification of MOFs active nanofillers while the modification agents can be involved in the process of ligand exchange. For example, polydopamine (PDA) is a versatile platform for surface modification of materials. Dopamine (DA), the monomer of PDA, is also capable of serving as a modulating ligand and coordinating with metal cations of the target MOFs. After the organic ligand is added, the DA will be replaced. Then, the DA monomer will self-polymerize into PDA and cover on the surface of the MOFs in the alkaline solution during the synthesis process [43]. This strategy is convenient for transforming the surface property of MOFs-based nano­ fillers from hydrophobic into hydrophilic due to the co-deposition of PDA, which is beneficial to the preparation of active nanofillers for waterborne coatings [60]. In addition, the exposed metal sites of MOFs can be converted into their oxides and hydroxides in some situation. For example, Ni–Co LDHs were in-situ synthesized on the surface of MSNs$\mathtt{B T A@Z I F}$ for improving the dispersibility of nanofillers in the water­ borne epoxy solution. The as-prepared MSNs-BTA $@$ ZIF-LDHs were much more hydrophilic than the MSNs-BTA@ZIF, due to the abundance of hydroxyl groups on the surface of Ni–Co LDHs [61].
## 2.3. MOFs-based nanofillers with both passive and active protective properties
In some harsh conditions, nanofillers with single passive or active protective function cannot endow the coating with durable protective performance. The latest researches demonstrated that nanofillers with both passive barrier and active self-healing properties are vital for the long-term protection performance of the coatings [62,63]. Yan et al. encapsulated benzotriazole (BTA) corrosion inhibitors in the ZIF-8 nanocontainers and the ZIF-8 containing BTA (ZB) was loaded on the surface of amino-functionalized two-dimensional $\mathrm{Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ $(\mathrm{f-Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x^{-}}}$ ZB). The composite anticorrosion coatings were prepared by incorpo rating $\mathbf{f-Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}\mathrm{-}Z B}$ into the epoxy matrix. Compared to the pure epoxy coating, the f-Ti3C2Tx-ZB/epoxy composite coatings exhibited one order of magnitude enhancement in coating resistance, revealing the better barrier effect of the $\mathbf{f-Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}-Z B},$ epoxy composite coatings. In addi­ tions, the low-frequency impedance value of $\mathbf{f}_{-}\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{T}_{\mathbf{x}^{-}}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{B},$ /epoxy com­ posite coating increased from $2.40\times10^{6}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (3 weeks of immersion) to $3.98\times10^{\bar{6}}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (4 weeks of immersion), which were attributed to the fact that the corrosion on the metal surface changed the local pH and triggered the release of BTA from $\mathrm{f-Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}\mathrm{-}Z B}$ , and then the BTA adsorbed at the defect to form a new protective coating. The passive protective property of $\mathbf{f}{\mathbf{-}}\mathrm{Ti}_{3}{\mathbf{C}}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathbf{x}}{\mathbf{-}}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{B}$ played a dominant role in the early immersion stage, which can reduce the diffusion paths of corrosion media, and the active self-healing property of $\mathrm{f-Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}\mathrm{-}Z B}$ avoid the fast failure of the composite coating (Fig. 7). Both passive and active pro­ tective properties of the composite coating contribute to the long-term protective performance [64]. A summary of the recently reported MOFs-based nanofillers is displayed in Table 1
## 3. Anticorrosion surfaces/coatings using MOFs as functional substrate support
MOFs can be directly used as the coating materials for various metal substrates. With the abundant active sites (functional groups), tunable size and morphology, and micro-nano structure of MOFs, MOFs coatings on the metal surfaces can serve as the functional substrate support for constructing various protective surfaces/coatings. Particularly, super­ hydrophobic surfaces (SHSs) and slippery liquid infused porous surfaces (SLIPSs) are two types of coatings/surfaces with potential in anticorro­ sion, and MOFs coatings facilitate the subsequent surface modification in the fabrication process of SHSs and SLIPSs.
## 3.1. Fabrication strategies of MOFs-based functional substrate support
Numerous methods have been implemented for constructing MOFs coatings on metallic substrates. Among them, in-situ growth and elec­ trochemical deposition are the more feasible approaches that are extensively used [67–69]. The MOFs coating can be generated through in-situ growth methods via direct nucleation of MOFs nanocrystals on the substrate or transforming the pre-prepared precursor coating into MOFs. In some of the pioneering works, the in-situ MOFs coatings were mainly fabricated by directly adding metal ions and organic linkers for nucleation and adsorption on the metal surface as synergetic corrosion inhibitors [70]. However, direct growth of MOFs on metal substrates by dip-coating methods may cause incompactness of the MOFs coatings because the nucleation and formation of MOFs are prior to the disper­ sion of MOF nanocrystals in the solution media. The densities of the generated MOFs nanocrystals on some substrates are relatively low, leading to the undesirable looseness of structures and decrease in adhesion [71]. Metal ions dissolved from the substrate surface can be directly utilized as the metallic binding points to address the problem of heterogeneous nucleation, which benefits the formation of compact MOFs coating with stronger adhesion to the substrates. For instance, Ji et al. fabricated a HKUST-1 $\mathrm{{(Cu_{3}(b t c)_{2}}}$ with b $\mathrm{tc}=1{,}3{,}5$ -benzene­ tricarboxylate) coating on the copper surface via in-situ one-step con­ version method (Fig. 8). Herein, the Cu substrate was served as both a conducting substrate and a source of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ for the formation of HKUST-1, which was realized via the simultaneously dissolution of Cu by oxidizing agent and the deprotonation of organic ligand by deprotonating agent. The obtained HKUST-1 coating was compact and the HKUST-1 crystals were well-intergrown. Similarly, this method was also employed to generate zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) coating on the zinc substrate [72]. A ZIF-90 coating was synthesized on the surface of $Z\mathrm{n-Al}$ alloy by an approach of dual zinc source, including the extrinsic zinc salt and the dissolved ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ from the substrate. The ZIF-90 coating exhibited compact structure and excellent adhesion property on the substrate surface [73].
In order to form densely packed MOFs coating, sufficient reactive sites for the growth of MOFs nanocrystals are highly desired. Premodifying the metallic substrates through surface treatments is an effective strategy to introduce abundant the reactive sites to the target substrates. Mussel-derived chemistry-based coating methods as one of the emerging surface modification strategies has also been implemented for the anchoring of MOFs. For instance, the catechol groups on poly­ dopamine (PDA) film provide a large number of chelating sites for heterogeneous nucleation of MOFs nanocrystals and are advantageous to the stability and compactness of MOFs coatings Fig. 8 [18].
As for some specific metal substrates, it is available to grow a pre­ cursor layer on the surface first, such as $\mathtt{Z n A l}$ layered double hydroxides (ZnAl-LDHs) on aluminum [74] and $\mathrm{Cu(OH)_{2}}$ nanowires (NWs) on copper [75]. The precursor layer usually serves as a sacrificial template to provide the metallic cores for the subsequent growth of MOFs coating. After that, the MOFs crystals can in-situ grow on the precursor layer. Zhang et al. utilized the ZnAl-CO3layered double hydroxide (LDH) formed by hydrothermal method as the precursor buffer layer. Then the $\mathsf{Z n A l-C O_{3}}$ LDH buffer layer acted as zinc source that partially converted into ZIF-8 coating. The as-prepared ZIF-8 coating exhibited strong adhesion to the aluminum substrate, because the ${\mathrm{ZnAl-CO}}_{3}$ LDH layer was constructed based on the aluminum ions provided by the substrate, and the ZIF-8 was generated using the zinc from $\mathrm{ZnAl-CO_{3}}$ LDH [76]. Jiang et al. proposed a more substrate-independent method using a ZnO coating as the precursor layer for the subsequent growth of ZIF-8 (Fig. 8). The adhesion of the internal $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ layer to the substrate was strong and the surface morphology of the ZIF-8 coating layer could be accurately regulated through adjusting the ligand concentration, syn­ thesis time, and temperature [77].
Electrochemical synthesis is another efficient way for fabricating MOFs coating on metallic surface. There are two typical electrochemical synthesis approaches (cathodic and anodic deposition) according to the different deposition sites of MOFs coating. In the process of cathodic deposition, the electrolyte solution mainly consists of metal salt, organic linker, and pro-base (e.g., $\mathrm{Et_{3}N H^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{H_{2}O}\mathrm{\overline{{.}}}$ ) [78]. An appropriate potential is applied to induce the growth of MOFs on the cathode. The reduction of the pro-base increases the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\pH}}$ value at the micro-environment in the vicinity of the cathode, inducing the deprotonation of the organic linkers, which then coordinate with metal ions to form the MOFs coating. Cathodic deposition is a substrate-independent method which can be applied on various types of metal surfaces. In the anodic elec­ trochemical synthesis route of MOFs, the potential is first implemented on the specific metal electrode to initiate the dissolution of metal sub­ strate and release of the metal ions. Subsequently, the metal ions coor­ dinate with the organic linker to form a MOFs layer on the electrode. With the variation of reaction conditions, the morphology of MOFs film can be highly regulated, which is different from the cathodic synthesis that the crystal morphology of MOFs is relatively fixed [67,79]. Later et al. prepared a mixed-ligand $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOFs coating on the Zn sub­ strate under room temperature via an anodic electrochemical synthesis method. It was found that the time of deposition and current were sig­ nificant factors influencing the purity and the yield of the MOFs crystals [80]. Moreover, the surfactant-assisted electrophoretic deposition method shows great potential in constructing hydrophobic MOFs coatings. The film of MOFs nanocrystals embedded with cetyl­ trimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) surfactant was deposited onto the copper surface by a cathodic electrochemical deposition, benefiting from the positive charges of CTAB (Fig. 9). The CTAB $@$ MOFs coating exhibited superior hydrophobicity due to the existence of hydrophobic tails consisting of alkyl groups [81].
## 3.2. MOFs as the textured substrate for superhydrophobic surfaces (SHSs)
Constructing superhydrophobic surfaces (SHSs) with a contact angle greater than $150^{\circ}$ and a sliding angle less than $10^{\circ}$ on the metal sub­ strates have been proven to be an effective anticorrosion strategy, especially in the marine environment. The underlying metal substrate covered by SHSs can be isolated from the external aggressive media due to the outstanding water repellency of SHSs. Surface wettability of a liquid droplet on a specific solid surface could be characterized by contact angle (CA) measurement. The static contact angle of a sessile droplet on an ideal smooth, chemically homogenous, rigid and insoluble solid surface could be determined by Young's equation (Eq. (1)) [82,83]:
$$
c o s\theta=\frac{\gamma_{S V}-\gamma_{S L}}{\gamma_{L V}}
$$
where γSL, γSV and $\gamma_{L V}$ stand for the interfacial tension acting between the solid-liquid, solid-vapor and liquid-vapor interfaces, respectively. The wetting schematic diagram of Young's equation is shown in Fig. 10(a). The surface tension of water droplet and air are usually constant, thus, a decrease in the surface tension of solid substrate gives rise to an increase in the static contact angle. The water contact angle (WCA) on a smooth surface modified by the hydrophobic moieties (e.g. alkyl, fluorine, aromatic groups) could only reach up to about $120^{\circ}$ , which does not meet the requirement of superhydrophobic surfaces [84].
Further, the models of wetting state on a textured surface were proposed by Wenzel, Cassie, and Baxter. Wenzel took the effects of surface roughness into consideration and proposes that the actual sur­ face area of a textured substrate is larger than the apparent surface area, where the droplet completely fills the voids on the structural surface, as displayed in Fig. 10(b). Surface roughness factor $r$ was incorporated into Wenzel's equation [85,86]:
$$
c o s{\theta}^{*}=r\frac{\gamma_{S V}-\gamma_{S L}}{\gamma_{L V}}=r c o s{\theta}
$$
Wenzel's theory is valid in homogeneous wetting state which occurs when the liquid droplet occupies the roughness grooves. However, Wenzel's theory is not suitable for heterogeneous surfaces. Cassie and Baxter formulated an equation to elaborate the apparent contact angle for heterogeneous surface with two chemical composition [87]:
$$
c o s\theta^{*}=f_{1}c o s\theta_{1}+f_{2}c o s\theta_{2}
$$
where $f_{\boldsymbol{{1}}}$ and $f_{2}$ are the area fractions $(f_{1}+f_{2}=1)\quad$ , $\theta_{1}$ and $\theta_{2}$ are the intrinsic contact angles. The schematic of Cassie-Baxter model is shown in the Fig. 10(c), the air is trapped in the roughness grooves, therefore, the liquid droplet only contacts with the solid surface at the top of protuberances. The intrinsic contact angle of a liquid droplet on the air layer is $180^{\circ}$ , so the trapped air in the textured substrate act as the liquid-repellent component. Accordingly, the apparent contact angle on a heterogeneous surface which is made up of air and solid can be calculated by [88]:
$$
c o s\theta^{*}=f c o s\theta+(1-f)c o s180^{\circ}=f c o s\theta+f-1
$$
Additionally, by taking the roughness factor r into consideration, the Cassie-Baxter equation is modified to the equation [10,89]:
$$
c o s{\theta}^{*}=r f c o s{\theta}+f-1
$$
According to the modified Cassie-Baxter equation, increase in sur­ face roughness could increase the amount of air trapped in the grooves and decrease $f,$ which leads to the increase in contact angle.
Based on Cassie-Baxter model, the construction of rough surface with micro-nano structure and modification with low surface energy reagents should be involved in preparation of SHSs, which can be achieved simultaneous or separately [89,90]. There are numerous approaches for the fabrication of SHSs on metal substrates, such as etching [91], solu­ tion immersion [92–94], electrochemical deposition [95–97], aluminum anodic oxidation (AAO) [98,99], micro-arc oxidation (MAO) [100], coating methods [101], hydrothermal treatment, and so on [102,103]. In the fabrication process of SHSs, many hydrophobic functionalized nanoparticles have been utilized to modify the substrate surface [104], such as $\mathrm{{siO}_{2}}$ [105,106], $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [107], ZnO [108], $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [109]. Most recently, many MOFs-based SHSs have been developed to accomplish superior water repellency and corrosion protection perfor­ mance, benefiting from the facile synthesis, tunable morphology and size, and easy modification of MOFs. Moreover, the robustness and stability of the MOFs-based-SHSs would benefit the long-term protection which makes MOFs type of idea candidate materials for the scalable manufacture in the future.
As displayed in Fig. 10(d), the SHSs can be generated from three routes, (1) the pre-roughing and hydrophobic post-treatment, (2) pretreatment with hydrophobic agents and post-roughing, and (3) in-situ preparation. It is worth noting that the in-situ preparation in this sec­ tion is referred to the direct fabrication of MOFs-based SHSs on metal surface by one-step treatment, while the in-situ preparation in Section 3.1 represents the in-situ growth of MOFs substrate support on the metal surface. Direct immersion of substrate in the MOFs solution mixed with hydrophobic modification reagents is an in-situ and facile method for generating SHSs on the metal surface. However, MOFs are a series of supramolecular soft materials with relatively poor mechanical strength, it is difficult to meet the requirement of mechanical robustness by directly using MOFs nanoparticles. In order to enhance the MOFs nanoparticles, Li et al. reported an enforcement strategy that micropo­ rous MOFs nanocrystals were modified with hard mesoporous silica (mSiO2). The $\mathrm{\msiO_{2}}$ -enclosed ZIF-8 $\mathrm{(ZIF{-}8@{m S i O_{2}})}$ ) exhibits approxi­ mately double hardness as the pure ZIF-8 nanoparticles, thus, ZIF$8@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ is more suitable for the construction of SHSs with mechanic stability [54]. A SHS on AZ31 Mg alloy prepared via etching and solu­ tion immersion methods has been developed by Wu and co-workers (Fig. 11A). The etched $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ substrate with micro-roughness has been immersed in the solution of hexadecyltrimethoxysilan (HDTMS)-modi­ fied ZIF- $8@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ and n-hexane to obtain a SHS. Comparing with bare $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ alloy, the substrate with the SHS showed impressively anticorrosion property. Moreover, the stronger chemical and mechanical stability of ZIF- ${\cdot8@{\cal S}\mathrm{i}{\sf O}_{2}}$ make the as-prepared SHS a more durable protective layer for the long-term protection against corrosion [110].
To obtain a robust SHS, another key point is to strengthen the adhesion of superhydrophobic layer to the substrate [111]. More recently, Jiang et al. prepared a ZIF-8-based MAO composite super­ hydrophobic coating (MZS) on a Mg substrate (Fig. 11B). The MAO layer was selected as a transition layer for the composite coating. Then, a ZnO layer was constructed on the MAO via a solvothermal method, which served as the zinc source for the subsequent growth of ZIF-8 coating. Finally, the $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ layer was in-situ transformed into ZIF-8 and the ZIF-8 substrate support was further modified by stearic acid (SA) to obtain the superhydrophobic coating (MZS-2). For comparison, the super­ hydrophobic coating prepared by the single-stage growth of ZIF-8 (without ZnO interlayer) was also prepared (MZS-1). The results from wetting state measurements and electrochemical tests suggested that both MZS-1 and MZS-2 showed superhydrophobicity and enhanced anticorrosion performance for the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ substrate. However, the stability and anticorrosion performance of MZS-2 was much better than MZS-1, because the ZIF-8 substrate support in MZS-2 formed by metal oxideinduced transformation has excellent adhesion to the MAO surface and the well-ordered protruding hierarchical structure could trap a large amount of air [112].
Previous research found that the robustness of SHSs formed by hy­ drophobic nanoparticles could be enhanced by a polymeric binder. The nanoparticles can be embedded into the polymeric coating which reduced the probability of being removed by external impacts [113–115]. For example, Wei et al. proposed a binary coating by combining a conventional polymeric material and a superamphiphobic nanoparticle. A traditional epoxy coating was firstly sprayed and cured on the surface of metallic substrate which simultaneously acts as a protective film and a binding layer of the superamphiphobic nano­ particles. Then, the HDTMS and 1H,1H,2H,2Hperfluorodecyltriethoxysilane (PFDTES)-functionalized $\mathrm{{siO}_{2}}$ were separately sprayed on the epoxy coating to form the SHSs. The com­ posite coating showed durable superhydrophobicity and anticorrosion performance during the long-term immersion period [116]. The MOFsbased SHSs could be constructed in the similar way. ZIF-8 as an exten­ sively investigated MOF has been used for fabricating diverse functional surfaces and coatings due to its stability in many cases. It is reported that ZIF-8 modified with 1H,1H,2H,2H perfluorooctyltriethoxysilane (POTS) turned to be superhydrophobic because of the micro-nano roughness of ZIF-8 and the low surface energy of POTS. Nevertheless, ZIF-8/POTS SHSs is easily to be removed and lose the superhydrophobicity under abrasion and impact. Chen et al. proposed a strategy using an epoxy resin (EP) bottom coating for enhancing the bonding performance of the ZIF-8/POTS layer (Fig. 11C). The double-layered superhydrophobic coatings exhibited their superior anticorrosion performance and robustness in various testing environments [117]. Meanwhile, Jin et al. used polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) as the bonding, adhesive material, and hydrophobilization agent for ZIF- $8@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2},$ and the ZIF ${-8@\mathsf{S i O}_{2}}/$ PDMS suspension was then applied on the Al surface by spin-coating to generate the SHS (Fig. 11D). The electrochemical results revealed that the SHS could durably protect Al substrate in the $3.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ NaCl solution [118].
To prolong the lifespan of MOFs-based anticorrosion SHSs, apart from enhancing the mechanical strength of MOFs particles and adhesion of MOFs coatings to the substrate, another significant strategy is to endow the SHSs with self-healing properties. It is feasible to endow an anticorrosion SHS with self-healing-based protective performance from two aspects: repairing the superhydrophobicity of surface or releasing corrosion inhibitors to form a new protective film on the exposed sub­ strate [119,120]. For example, Wang et al. constructed a self-healing SHS by spraying a perluoroalkyl mechacrylic copolymer and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles composite on the substrate that was precoated with PDMS. While the SHS was destroyed, the PDMS storing in the micro-nano structure can migrate to the surface via a heat treatment and repair the superhydrophobicity [121]. Fu et al. synthesized tungstate $(\mathrm{WO}_{4}^{2-})$ - intercalated LDHs conversion coating on $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ alloy and then the surface was further functionalized by ureido-crosslinked polydimethylsiloxane (U-PDMS) and laurate-modified LDHs to obtain a self-healing SHS. When the coating was intact, the superhydrophobicity of the top layer was the dominant factor of corrosion protection via blocking the aqueous corrosive media and superhydrophobic layer also avoided the leakage of corrosion inhibitors from underlying LDHs conversion layer. When the defects were introduced to the SHS, the $\mathrm{WO}_{4}^{2-}$ released by ion exchange process of the LDHs conversion film turned to become the dominant protective component [122]. MOFs with tunable cavities and pores are promising candidates of storage materials for constructing selfhealing SHSs. MOFs coating developed from mussel-inspired dopamine chemistry has also been used for fabrication of self-healing SHSs. For instance, Cai et al. fabricated a hydrophobic ZIF-90 coating on the sur­ face of PDA, followed by the treatment of the fluoro-containing polymer. The fluoroalkylsilane moieties were preserved in the porous structure of the MOFs coating which endowed the coating with self-repairable superhydrophobicity [123].
3.3. MOFs as the nano-reservoir of lubricant for slippery liquid infused porous surfaces (SLIPSs)
Slippery liquid infused porous surfaces (SLIPSs) inspired by Nepen­ thes pitcher plant have attracted enormous attention in corrosion pro­ tection application due to their superior liquid repellency of the infused lubricant which can form a barrier layer to block the propagation of corrosive media [104]. In general, SLIPSs are composed of two parts: a porous and textured substrate with appropriate surface tension and a lubricant layer with low surface tension. The fabrication process of SLIPSs on metal substrates normally includes three steps: the substrate is first treated to generate a hierarchical surface structure, the structural
Fig. 11. (A) Fabrication of ZIF- $8@S_{1}^{:}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ micro/nano hierarchical superhydrophobic surface on AZ31 magnesium alloy: immersing the etched $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ substrate in the solution of hexadecyltrimethoxysilane (HDTMS) modified ZIF- ${\cdot8@\mathsf{S i O}_{2}}$ and n-hexane [110]. (Reproduced with permission from ref. [110]. Copy­ right 2017 American Chemical Society.) (B) the schematic illustration of the preparation of ZIF-8- based micro-arc oxidation (MAO) composite super­ hydrophobic coating (MZS). Top: the in-situ growth of ZIF-8 coating on MAO surface and the hydrophobic modification (MZS-1); Bottom: a homologous metal oxide $\left(\mathrm{ZnO}\right)$ coating was first prepared on the MAO surface, and the ZIF-8 coating was synthesized using $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ as the zinc source and subsequently modified by hydrophobic agents (MZS-2) [112]. (Reproduced with permission from ref. [112]. Copyright 2021 Elsevier B.V.) (C) The fabrication process of epoxy (EP) coating, superhydrophobic ZIF-8/POTS coating and ZIF-8/POTS/EP coating. PA and POTS are polyamide curing agent and 1H,1H,2H,2H per­ fluorooctyltriethoxysilane, respectively [117]. (Reproduced with permission from ref. [117]. Copy­ right 2020 Elsevier B.V.) (D) Schematic illustration of the preparation route of ZIF- $8@{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ /PDMS super­ hydrophobic surface on an Al substrate, CTAB and TEOS represent cetyltrimethylammonium bromide and tetraethyl orthosilicate, respectively [118]. (Reproduced with permission from ref. [118]. Copy­ right 2021 American Chemical Society.)
substrate is then modified to achieve appropriate surface energy, and finally, the lubricant is infused and immobilized in the pores of the structured substrate by capillary interaction. Compared with SHSs, the SLIPSs usually display strong mechanical stability and are regarded as a potential alternative to SHSs.
Compared to some other textured substrates for SLIPSs, metal organic frameworks (MOFs) functional substrate support can provide abundant space for the storage of lubricants (Fig. 12) due to the intrinsic pores of MOFs and the surface of MOFs is easily to be further modifi­ cation. Moreover, the synthesis method of MOFs nanoparticles and coating are usually facile and mild which can minimize the damage to the metal substrate during the preparation process. Additionally, the choice of substrate is much more diverse owing to the preparation process of MOFs substrate supports that incorporates the flexibility of metal ions and organic linkers as well as the selectivity of functional groups.
Similar to the fabrication of MOFs-based SHSs, all preparation stra­ tegies of MOFs-based substrate support mentioned in Section 3.1 can be applied in the fabrication of MOFs-based SLIPSs. For instance, Fang et al. reported a Cu-CAT-1-based SLIPS by a two-step conversion method. The copper substrate was first oxidized into $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ nanowires (NWs), the $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ NWs were subsequently served as the Cu source for the in-situ growth of Cu-CAT-1 MOFs (Fig. 13). Compared with the $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ NWsbased SLIPS, the Cu-CAT-1-derived SLIPS exhibited more excellent sta­ bility and replenishment performance owing to the nanochannels of CuCAT-1 providing larger space for the storage of lubricant [75]. A SLIPS prepared by surface-grown hydrophilic MOFs was proposed by Gao and co-workers. The $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ film was grown on a carboxyl groupsfunctionalized surface with the assistance of irradiation and then the $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ layer was infused with silicon oil to generate a slippery surface which exhibited excellent stability, water repellency, and antiicing performance [68]. Some native oxide layers on metallic sub­ strates can also be utilized as self-sacrifice templates for the growth of porous MOFs support. Li et al. used the oxide film on the aluminum sheet as the aluminum source, in-situ hydrothermally synthesized porous MIL110 coating on the aluminum surface, and the SLIPS was obtained by direct infusion of lubricant. The Tafel polarization curves have proven that the lubricant-infused MIL-110 surface can significantly improve the corrosion resistance of aluminum sheets and the corrosion current density decreased from $2.627\times10^{-7}\mathrm{A/cm}^{2}$ (aluminum sheet) to 1.285 $\times~10^{-8}\mathrm{A/cm^{2}}$ [124].
The stability of the lubricant layer on surface of SLIPSs is critical factor for the durability of SLIPSs. Although the lubricant layer of SLIPSs is more stable than the air cushion trapped in SHSs due to the higher density and viscosity, the lubricant is easily to be replaced when it contacts with corrosion media, especially under dynamic environments like hydrodynamic shear. Meanwhile, the evaporation of lubricant is also inevitable which is originated from the intrinsic property of the lubricant [125,126]. The displacement and evaporation of the trapped lubricant cause the depletion of its reserves and finally result in the loss of water repellency. Covalent grafting strategy that immobilizes the micro-nano reservoir or lubricant molecules on the substrate have been introduced to fabricate robust slippery surfaces [127–129]. For instance, the Zr-based MOFs can be covalently immobilized on the APTESmodified glass surface by an amide linkage that formed by the reac­ tion between amino groups on the surface and carboxyl groups in the organic ligands, and the post-functionalization with alkyl silanes rendered the surface amphiphobic (Fig. 14) [130]. However, the repellency performance of the covalently-grafted slippery surface was inferior compared to the traditional lubricant-infused surfaces [131]. Combination of lubricant infusion and strong covalent anchoring can simultaneously endow a surface with excellent slippery performance and robustness [132,133]. The MOFs that contain organic linkers with functional groups at edge are promising candidates for the fabrication of SLIPSs that integrate covalent grafting and lubricant infusion. UiO-66- $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ was directly introduced on an irradiation-treated polypropylene surface. After the modification with 3-glycidyloxypropyl-trimethoxysi­ lane (KH560) via the reaction between amino groups of UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ and epoxy group of KH560, the hydrogen-terminated silicon fluid was immobilized on the KH560-modified surface and the micronanochannels of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ coating. Dual immobilization of lubri­ cant significantly strengthened the robustness of the slippery surface [134].
## 4. Characterizing the anticorrosion performance and mechanisms of MOFBSCs via electrochemical techniques
Various electrochemical techniques like open circuit potential (OCP), electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), Tafel polariza­ tion curves, scanning Kelvin probe (SKP), scanning vibrating electrode technique (SVET), scanning electrochemical microscope (SECM), and local electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (LEIS) have been applied in the investigation of anticorrosion performance and protective mechanisms for organic polymer coatings incorporated with nanofillers, inorganic barrier coatings, SHSs, and SLIPSs. These techniques are also adapted to MOFBSCs.
## 4.1. Open circuit potential (OCP)
OCP gives the state of thermodynamic stability of the tested material surface in the particular immersion condition. Variation of the oxidation tendency of the sample surface results in potential fluctuation. In gen­ eral, OCP is relatively positive at the early stage of immersion and gradually shifts negatively with the increase of immersion time. The more positive and steadier OCP indicates the better protective perfor­ mance of the surface or coating. When OCP technique is conducted on SHSs, there will be a large fluctuation of potential due to the air cushion on the surface. For the coatings with self-healing performance, the positive shift of OCP usually indicates the repair of the protective properties of the coatings.
## 4.2. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS)
EIS is a versatile method for evaluating the corrosion resistance of materials and protective performance of coatings. In the EIS measure­ ment, a low-amplitude alternating current (AC) signal of a sine voltage or current will be applied to the coated metallic substrate with a threeelectrode system. The frequency range of EIS measurement for anti­ corrosion evaluation usually varies from $0.01\ \mathrm{Hz}$ to $10^{5}\mathrm{Hz}$ . One of the standard representations of EIS results is the Bode plot, including Bode modulus plot and Bode phase angle plot. The advantage of Bode plot is that the variation of impedance modulus can be exhibited prominently. Commonly, the impedance at the low frequency $(|Z|_{0.01\mathrm{Hz}})$ in the Bode modulus plot is a semi-quantitative indicator of the protective perfor­ mance of the surface/coating. The higher $|Z|_{0.01\mathrm{Hz}}$ indicates the better protection property of the surface/coating. Another type of represen­ tation of EIS data is Nyquist plot, in which the features including the formation of new interfacial in the coating and diffusion state of media can be represented clearly [135,136].
C. Zhou et al.
The EIS results can be utilized to deduce the equivalent electric cir­ cuits (EEC) of the electrode, by which the kinetic process and the un­ derlying protective mechanism can be unveiled. Relevant element parameters in the equivalent circuit, such as solution resistance $R_{s},$ coating resistance $R_{c},$ , charge transferring resistance $R_{c t},$ coating capac­ itance $C_{c},$ and double layer capacitance $C_{d l},$ can be estimated through fitting EIS data. In the cases of SHSs and SLIPSs, $R_{c}$ is usually replaced by the resistance of rough layer $R_{r}.$
Most organic coatings represent a typical capacitive behavior at the early stage of immersion, where the coating acts as an intact barrier against corrosive media. At this stage, only one time constant can be observed, which is related to the interface of solution/coating, and the EIS data can be fitted by the EEC shown in Fig. 15A, where $Q_{c}$ is the constant phase element used for substituting the $C_{c}$ , because of the interfacial heterogeneity. The relationship of capacitance and constant phase element is as below:
$$
C_{x}=\left(Q_{x}\times R_{x}^{1-n}\right)^{\frac{1}{n}}
$$
Once the coating is damaged and the corrosion media arrive at the interface of coating/substrate, a new interface of solution/substrate generates. The second time constant can be detected from the Bode phase angle plot and Nyquist plot. At this stage, the corrosion occurs at the metal surface, and the EIS data can be fitted with EEC in Fig. 15B and C. Among them, EEC in Fig. 15B is usually applied to evaluate the coating systems that the corrosion media permeate the coating and arrive at the coating/metal interface through micro-pores, and the blister areas are localized and corresponded to the micro-pores. If the corrosion media homogeneously permeates into the coating and the corrosion cells distribute uniformly, the EIS results can be fitted via Fig. 15C [137–139].
As for SHSs, at the early stage of immersion, there are usually three time-constants due to the existence of air cushion on the surface, and the EEC is modified (shown in Fig. 15D). With the increase of immersion time, the air cushion of SHSs gradually deteriorates and the number of time-constants will decrease from three to two, and the EEC is corre­ sponding to Fig. 15E [140]. As shown in Fig. 15F, the EEC of SLIPSs usually displays three time-constants, which is similar to the situation of SHSs while the air cushion of SHSs is replaced by the lubricant layer.
Element parameters in the equivalent circuit could give more detailed information to help understand the state of protective coating/ surface and the process of corrosion. $R_{c}$ and $C_{c}$ are usually utilized to evaluate the barrier effect of the protective coatings/films which was undermined during the whole immersion time due to the penetration of corrosion media. $R_{c}$ is a property of the material of the coating/film and varies with the thickness and composition of the coating/film. $C_{c}$ is a parameter to determine the water uptake of the coating/film [141]. In general, the deterioration of barrier property of the protective coatings/ films results in the decrease of $R_{c}$ and increase of $C_{c}$ . Furthermore, water absorption rate is another reflection of the barrier effect of coatings, which can be calculated by the Brasher and Kingsbury (BK) equation [7,142]:
$$
X_{\nu}\%=\frac{l o g\Big(\frac{C_{c}(t)}{C_{c}(0)}\Big)}{l o g(80)}\times100\
$$
where $C_{c}(t),C_{c}(O),$ , and $X_{\nu}(\%)$ are $C_{c}$ at time t, $C_{c}$ at initial time and the volume fraction of water in coating matrix, respectively. $R_{c t}$ could be utilized for assessment of corrosion reactivity and tendency, which directly reflects the kinetics of the electrochemical corrosion reaction at the metal/coating interface. The high $R_{c t}$ value is a reflection that the redox reaction at the metal/coating interface is inhibited. $C_{d l}$ is related to the metal area contacted with the corrosion media, which could reflect the degree of degradation and cracks of the coatings/films at the immersion time. The coatings/films with many micro-cracks and pores usually exhibit a high value of $C_{d l},$ and a low value of $C_{d l}$ indicates the excellent impermeability of the coating/film [143,144].
## 4.3. Tafel polarization curves
Corrosion system of metallic substrate is composed of an anodic re­ action and a cathodic reaction. Butler-Volmer equation [145,146] and the mixed potential theory [147] give the basic kinetic equation for metallic corrosion in aqueous electrolyte, as shown in Eq. (8) [148]:
$$
i=i_{c o r r}\bigg\{e x p\bigg[\frac{2.303(E-E_{c o r r})}{\beta_{a}}\bigg]-e x p\bigg[-\frac{2.303(E-E_{c o r r})}{\beta_{c}}\bigg]\bigg\}
$$
where $E_{c o r r},~i_{c o r r},~E,$ and i are corrosion potential, corrosion current density, the potential applied on the polarized corrosion system, and the external current density, respectively. $\beta_{a}$ and $\beta_{c}$ are anodic Tafel slope and cathodic Tafel slope, respectively. When $E$ is far away from $E_{c o r r},$ Eq. (8) can be recast as Eq. (9) which is known as Tafel equation.
$$
E=a\pm b l o g|i|
$$
where a is a constant, b is identical to $\beta_{a}$ or $\beta_{c}$ . The Tafel equation demonstrates that the logarithm of external current density alters line­ arly with the potential at high overpotential while $i_{c o r r}$ could be obtained by extrapolating the tangent of $E\sim\log|i|$ to the $E_{c o r r}.$
The Tafel polarization is an effective way to evaluate corrosion resistance of the protective film-coated metal substrate. $E_{c o r r}$ is a parameter that indicates the thermodynamic tendency of corrosion occurring on the metal surface that is negatively correlated to corrosion tendency. $i_{c o r r}$ is a direct reflection of corrosion rate on the metal surface, and a lower $i_{c o r r}$ usually reveals better corrosion protection for the metal substrate. Furthermore, the protection efficiency (PE), corrosion rate (CR), and polarization resistance $(R_{p})$ of MOFBSCs-coated metal sub­ strate can be calculated by the equation below [149]:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{P E_{c o r t}^{q_{c}}=100\times\left(\frac{\left(i_{c o r r,b l a n k}-i_{c o r r}\right)}{i_{c o r r,b l a n k}}\right)}\\ &{R_{p}=\frac{\beta_{a}\beta_{c}}{2.303\left(\beta_{a}+\beta_{c}\right)i_{c o r r}}}\\ &{C R=3270\times\frac{i_{c o r r}M}{V d}}\end{array}
$$
Where $i_{c o r r.},b l a n k$ is the corrosion current density of the bare substrate, $i_{c o r r}$ is the corrosion current density of the substrate with surface treat­ ment or coating, 3270 is a constant, $\quad V,d,$ , and $M$ are the valence, the density of substrate, and the relative atomic mass, respectively.
## 4.4. Localized electrochemical techniques
SKP is a non-destructive electrochemical technique for detecting corrosion process at both metal surface and delaminated interfaces of the coated samples. During the measurement, a vibrating capacitor probe (Kelvin probe) is used to measure its Volta potential difference $\bar{(\varphi_{s a m p l e}^{r e f})}$ between the sample and reference electrode, and the working function of the sample could be easily converted and obtained. The corrosion potential $(E_{c o r r})$ of metal substrate can be converted as a function of Volta potential difference as shown in equation below [150]:
$$
E_{c o r r}=\varphi_{s a m p l e}^{r e f}+c o n s t a n t
$$
Generally, electric potential on inhomogeneous metal surface later­ ally varies and $\varphi_{s a m p l e}^{r e f}$ (equal to the contact potential difference (CPD) on the substrate surface) usually changes across different sites of the metal surface.
For evaluating self-healing anticorrosion performance of coatings, SKP measurements are usually conducted on the sample surface with an artificial scratch. Liu et al. adopted the SKP technique for estimating the self-healing protection property of the waterborne epoxy coating with modified $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocontainers. The test was carried out on the sample with a scratch of $3\mathrm{mm}$ length and $0.1\mathrm{mm}$ width for different immersion time. The maximal CPD for blank coating rose from $0.36\mathrm{~V~}$ to $0.45{\mathrm{~V}}_{.}$ , which indicated that the corrosion tendency on the pure epoxy-coated steel was intensified due to the degradation of the coating, while the maximal CPD for $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocontainer composite coating dropped from $0.27\mathrm{~V~}$ to $0.20\mathrm{~V~}$ , revealing the corrosion inhibition effect of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocontainers for the beneath steel substrate [151]. Most recently, Zhou et al. used SKP for investigating the self-healing performance of HMSN-BTA $\scriptstyle{\mathcal{Q}}\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ active nanofillers epoxy composite coatings. The asobtained SKP maps showed that the CPD for HMSN-BTA@ZIF-8/epoxy coating was much smaller than that for the pure epoxy coating, revealing the notable corrosion inhibition of HMSN-BTA@ZIF-8/epoxy coating [57]. In addition, the permeation of corrosive solution into the interface of coating/metal will lead to deterioration of interfacial adhesion and the delamination degree of the coatings can also be analyzed by SKP. Since cathodic delamination always initiates at defects within the coating, accordingly, a sudden decline of the potential in the SKPs maps is the reflection of the delamination front [152]. Qiu et al. utilized SKP technique to investigate the coating delamination of the model coatings containing only topcoat, UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{rGO}$ , and gallic acid loaded UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}@r G O}$ (GA-UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{rGO}_{\j}^{\j}$ . As shown in the Fig. 16, the potential as a function of the distance (vertical direction) to the artificial scratch was recorded. For the model coating containing only topcoat, the delamination range was up to $4000\upmu\mathrm{m}$ within $600\mathrm{{min}}$ and the potential at the delamination areas was close to the corrosion potential of bare steel. For the coating containing UiO-66- ${\cdot{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@\mathbf{r}\mathsf{G O}}$ and topcoat, the $4000\ {\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ of delamination emerged after $1080\ \mathrm{min}$ and the potential at the delamination areas was higher than that of the case only containing topcoat, suggesting that the corrosion activity was restricted by the UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{r}\mathbf{G}\mathbf{O}/$ topcoat forming a hierarchical barrier to resist the corrosive media. For the coating containing GA-UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{rGO}$ and topcoat, the delamination degree further decreased, and the potential in the delamination areas rebounded with prolonging the immersion time, proving the self-healing effect of GA corrosion in­ hibitors [36].
SVET is an efficient micro-region electrochemical method to inves­ tigate localized corrosion activity on the metal surface by taking advantage of the high sensitivities to local current distribution. The anodic region in SVET map is corresponded to the metal dissolution and the further hydrolysis, while the cathodic region refers to the oxygen reduction process. When the protective film on the metal surface is cracked, many micro-anodic and cathodic regions will appear on the substrate surface along with the corrosion reaction. With the increase of immersion time, the anodic and cathodic activities usually become more intense, revealing the acceleration of corrosion on the substrate. If there are some healing agents incorporated in the coating, after a certain duration of immersion, the micro-region corrosion activities will be restricted and the micro-region current density on the substrate will decrease. The changes of micro-region current density can directly reveal the inhibition effect of the healing agents [153].
As mentioned in Section 2.1, some MOFs are applied in the insulation of conductive nanofiller for preventing galvanic corrosion. SVET is also an essential technique for studying the electrochemical behaviors of coated samples with scratches and has potential in investigation of insulation effect of MOFs [154]. For example, SVET was carried out for evaluating the corrosion process of the composite coating doping with lhistidine functionalized graphene (fG). After $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of immersion, the corrosion activity on the defected area of reduced graphene oxide (rGO) composite coating was aggravated, which was even more serious than the pure epoxy coating. The undesirable protection performance is partly attributed to the corrosion promotion activity of conductive graphene which caused the micro-galvanic corrosion of metal sub­ strates. However, corrosion activity at the scratched area of fG/epoxy coating revealed in the SVET map was much lower, which was ascribed to that the connection between graphene and metal surface was restrained by the attached l-histidine [24].
SLIPSs can spontaneously repair the scratches via the transfer of the lubricant from the porous area of the substrate. SVET can monitor the self-replenishment process and evaluate the healing effect via the change of local current density. The combination of different microregion electrochemical techniques is important for clearing out the un­ derlying anticorrosion mechanisms of MOFBSCs. For instance, Jiang et al. constructed a multilayered SLIPS with dual self-healing effects based on the sealing effect of lubricant and the automatic release of $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}$ anionic corrosion inhibitor. The healing effects of the lubricant and corrosion inhibitor were investigated by SKP and SVET (Fig. 17), respectively. Dynamic variation of the lateral Volta potential difference was recorded with a time interval of $40\mathrm{min}$ after scratching. At first, the potential at the defected area was more negative than that at the intact area, and the maximal ΔCPD was up to $1.15\mathrm{V}$ . With time going on, the trace of defect on the potential mapping gradually narrowed and almost disappeared. The maximal ΔCPD in the second and third testing decreased to $0.77{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and $0.52\mathrm{~V~}$ , respectively. All of these phenomena suggested that the self-replenishment of lubricant could almost completely seal the scratch at the defected area on the surface. In addition, the current density maps of each sample were obtained after 0.5, 8, and $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ of immersion in 0.05 M NaCl aqueous solution, respectively. As for the SLIPS, the initial current density was much lower than that of the contrast sample, probably due to the mobility of lubricant that repaired the scratch to some extent, which was aligned with SKP results. With the increase of time, the current density contin­ uingly declined and was close to zero after $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ of immersion, which could be attributed to the synergetic effect of the impermeable lubricant and corrosion inhibitor encapsulated in the LDH intermediate layer [155].
SECM can give a more microscopic evaluation of self-repairing effect at the defect area. In the most common corrosion process of metallic materials, oxygen dissolving in solution can participate the cathodic corrosion reaction directly. The microelectrode of SECM can detect the dissolved oxygen by immobilizing the potential in the range of diffusioncontrolled oxygen reduction in the cathodic reduction reaction below [156,157]:
$$
O_{2}(g)+2H_{2}O(l)+4e^{-}\rightarrow4O H^{-}(a q)
$$
Qian et al. performed SECM on the BTA-doped superhydrophobic surface with shape memory function (Fig. 18). For the blank coating without BTA corrosion inhibitors, the corrosion immediately occurred at the defect areas after 1 day of immersion. The current density of oxygen reduction process detected by the microelectrode decreased to almost $0\mathrm{A}$ , indicating that the local oxygen has been run out by the corrosion cathodic reaction. After 3 days of immersion, the SECM showed that the width of corrosion area increased, and the current density still main­ tained around $0\mathrm{{A}}$ , suggesting the absence of healing agents. A similar corrosion activity was observed for the superhydrophobic coating incorporated with $5\%$ BTA after the first day of immersion. However, the corrosion activity was significantly inhibited after 3 days of immersion. Consequently, with the increased oxygen remained, the current density detected by the microelectrode was increased up to about $8\times10^{-10}\dot{\mathrm{A}}.$ After the healing process of the shape memory matrix, no corrosion activity was detected on the defected area of the superhydrophobic coating after 1 day of immersion. Even though the corrosion activity reappeared at the scratch, the corrosion activity after 3 days of immersion was much lower than that in the cases of scratched BTA-free and BTA$5\%$ coatings [120].
LEIS is a powerful technique to investigate corrosion process on the bare or coated metallic substrates through the measurement of local alternating current (AC) current density above the metallic surface using two micro-electrodes of platinum. The AC potential difference relating to the AC current in the electrolyte measured between two microelectrodes. The AC solution current density $(i_{l o c a l})$ is then calculated by Ohm's law:
$$
i_{l o c a l}=\frac{V_{l o c a l}}{d}\kappa
$$
where $d,\ \kappa,$ and $V_{l o c a l}$ stand for the distance between two microelectrodes, conductivity of solution, and the AC potential difference between the two tips of the probe. By inputting a potential difference between the sample and reference electrode, the local impedance can be calculated by [158,159]:
$$
Z_{l o c a l}=\frac{\Delta V_{a p p l i e d}}{i_{l o c a l}}
$$
and the intensification of corrosion activity at the detected area could be clearly identified by the increase of the admittance (Y).
$$
Y_{l o c a l}=\frac{1}{Z_{l o c a l}}
$$
For evaluating self-healing protective property of the coatings, the application of LEIS technique was reported in many previous research. For instance, Liu et al. developed a smart anticorrosion coating with corrosion sensing and self-healing performances based on ZIF$7@$ polyethyleneglycol-tannic acid (ZIF-7@PEG-TA) nanocontainers (Fig. 19) [65]. After corrosion occurred, the loaded TA and benzimid­ azole (BIM) organic ligand could be released for sensing corrosion process and self-healing, respectively, due to the pH-responsive property of ZIF-7 $@$ PEG-TA. All samples were introduced an artificial scratch and immersed in $3.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ NaCl solution for different intervals. For pure epoxy coating, the LEIS admittance value at scratch area gradually increased from $1.56\times10^{-7}\left(2\mathrm{h}\right)$ to $2.36\times10^{-7}\Omega^{-1}\left(24\mathrm{h}\right)$ , suggesting the augmented corrosion reactivity. The admittance at defected area of ZIF-7/epoxy composite coating were kept at a low value (about $1.0\times$ $10^{-7}\Omega^{-1}\big)$ ) during the $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of immersion, indicating the restricted corrosion reactivity. Compared to the pure epoxy coating and ZIF-7/ epoxy composite coating, the ZIF-7 $@$ PEG-TA epoxy coating displayed the lowest admittance after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of immersion. Additionally, the defected area in the LEIS map for ZIF-7 $@$ PEG-TA epoxy coating grad­ ually narrowed with the increase of immersion time, which was attrib­ uted to the protective film formed by BIM and TA molecules. Both the variation of admittance value and defected area in the LEIS map gave the verification of self-healing anticorrosion properties for the smart coating in micro-scale. Moreover, the LEIS map can also reflect the delamination rate of the coating. Li et al. used LEIS to investigate the active protective properties of the pure epoxy coating and the composite coating con­ taining GO, ZIF-8/GO, and 2-Mercaptobenzimidazole (M) loaded ZIF-8/ graphene oxide nanofillers (M-ZIF-8/GO). Among them, the LEIS maps for the pure epoxy coating show that the corrosive ions diffused across the whole coating area, suggesting the severe coating delamination and poor interfacial adhesion between the pure epoxy and steel substrate. For the composite coating containing ZIF-8/GO, the diffusion of corro­ sive ions was restricted compared with that of the pure epoxy, sup­ porting the hypothesis that the incorporation of ZIF-8/GO could reinforce the interfacial binding force between the coating and metal substrate to suppress the rate of coating delamination [144].
## 5. Conclusion and outlook
In the work, the fabrication strategies, anticorrosion properties, and mechanisms of several types of metal organic frameworks-based sur­ faces and coatings have been systematically reviewed. The anticorrosion surfaces/coatings using MOFs-based nanofillers were first discussed. The MOFs-based passive nanofillers can decrease the porosity of the composite coatings and block the diffusion paths of aggressive media, such chloride anions, oxygen, and water. The MOFs-based active nanofillers are one type of nanocarrier with controllable release prop­ erty in response to external stimuli. When corrosion occurs at defects in the protective coating, these nanofillers can respond to external stimuli and automatically release healing agents, preventing the metallic sub­ strate from further damage. As for the anticorrosion surfaces/coatings using MOFs as functional substrate support, MOFs respectively serve as the textured substrate for superhydrophobic surfaces and nano-reservoir of lubricant for slippery liquid infused porous surfaces. The fundamental principles of various electrochemical techniques for characterizing the MOFBSCs were also discussed. The anticorrosion performance of MOFBSCs can be characterized by OCP, EIS, and Tafel curves, while the protective mechanisms of MOFBSCs are usually probed by the localized electrochemical techniques.
Although impressive progress has been achieved in the MOFs-based anticorrosion surfaces and coatings, some critical issues and technical challenges remain to be further explored. (1) The anticorrosion perfor­ mance of MOFs-based SLIPSs in practical harsh environments should be explored. (2) It remains unclear about the influences of the structures and chemical compositions of MOFs on the impermeability, corrosion inhibition effect, adhesion, and durability of the MOFBSCs. (3) Only limited number of studies have focused on the MOFBSCs with multiple protective functions, which should be further investigated. It is expected that the coatings coupled with both physical barrier, self-healing and superhydrophobicity properties tend to possess more comprehensive and durable protective effect. (4) Some organic linkers in the MOFs can intrinsically serve as corrosion inhibitors, and it remains unclear how these organic linkers influence the inhibition effects of the extrinsic corrosion inhibitors which are encapsulated in the MOFs-based active nanofillers. It is noted that the commercial use of MOFBSCs required more significant effort in various areas, such as improving the prepa­ ration methods of MOFs and MOFBSCs through the use of environmentfriendly, low-cost, and high-yield materials and technologies.
Overall, several types of MOFs-based anticorrosion surfaces and coatings have been successfully developed and functionalized on the surfaces of metallic materials. MOFs-based anticorrosion surfaces and coatings have exhibited great potential to inhibit corrosion in a wide range of working environment conditions. Further research on the pre­ cise design of the nanostructures of MOFs-based surfaces/coatings and their surface properties will facilitate the development of more advanced materials/surfaces for eliminating the challenging corrosion issues in numerous engineering and industrial processes.
